 "derivation": "French risque (16th c.) is certainly the immediate source of English risk and of modern Persian ریسک, but its own origin is not the one drawn here. Etymonline: 'risk (n.) 1660s, risque, hazard, danger, peril … from French risque (16c.), from Italian risco, riscio (modern rischio), from riscare \"run into danger\", a word of uncertain origin.' The derivation from Vulgar Latin *resecum/*resecāre 'to cut off' (hence 'rock that cuts ships') is the account of the 1969-76 American Heritage Dictionary that Nourai is quoting, but the current AHD has replaced it: 'French risque, from Italian risco, rischio, probably from Medieval Greek rizikon, sustenance obtained by a soldier through his own initiative, fortune, from Arabic rizq, sustenance, that which God allots, from Syriac rūzīqā, daily bread, from Middle Iranian rōčīg, from rōč, day, from Old Iranian *raučah-.' On that account risque has nothing to do with cutting at all — it is an Iranian word (rōz 'day' > 'daily bread') that travelled through Syriac, Arabic and Byzantine Greek into Italian. Either way the arrow Latin secāre → French risque is not supported by current scholarship.",
